<h2>1. Executive Summary & Problem Formulation</h2>
<p>
Modern multi-node retail supply chains face critical transactional failure modes when handling rapid omnichannel demand spikes. Standard web applications employing optimistic concurrency control or application-level stock checks suffer catastrophic race conditions during simultaneous customer checkouts, culminating in overselling, negative inventory levels, and unrecoverable database inconsistencies.
</p>
<div class="callout">
  <strong>Core Engineering Thesis:</strong>
  AtomicStock eliminates race conditions and partial ledger corruptions by utilizing PostgreSQL database-level pessimistic row-locking (<code>SELECT ... FOR UPDATE</code>) combined with atomic transaction rollbacks, sub-50ms Redis cache acceleration, real-time WebSocket telemetry, and role-based multi-tenant facility isolation.
</div>

<h2>2. Mathematical & Algorithmic Concurrency Model</h2>
<p>
Consider a high-velocity flash sale on SKU $S_i$ located at warehouse hub $W_j$ with physical available stock $Q = 1$. Under standard read-modify-write patterns, $N$ concurrent requests perform:
</p>
<ol>
  <li>$T_1$ reads $Q = 1$</li>
  <li>$T_2$ reads $Q = 1$ (interleaved read before $T_1$ commits)</li>
  <li>$T_1$ updates $Q \leftarrow Q - 1 = 0$ (SUCCESS)</li>
  <li>$T_2$ updates $Q \leftarrow Q - 1 = -1$ (FAILURE: Race condition resulting in overselling)</li>
</ol>
<p>
<strong>AtomicStock Pessimistic Serialization Mechanism:</strong>
When checkout transaction $T_1$ executes:
</p>
<pre>
BEGIN TRANSACTION;
SELECT * FROM "Inventory" 
WHERE "productId" = $productId AND "warehouseId" = $warehouseId 
FOR UPDATE;

-- Row lock acquired by T1. Parallel transactions T2...TN are queued at database engine level.
-- T1 evaluates condition:
IF quantity &lt; requestedQuantity THEN
   ROLLBACK;
   RETURN HTTP 409 CONFLICT;
ELSE
   UPDATE "Inventory" SET quantity = quantity - requestedQuantity;
   INSERT INTO "AuditTransaction" (type, quantity, ...) VALUES ('OUTBOUND', ...);
   COMMIT;
END IF;
</pre>
<p>
When $T_2$ acquires the released row lock, the updated quantity is verified as $0$. $T_2$ immediately aborts and returns an explicit <code>HTTP 409 Conflict: Insufficient Stock</code> without any corrupt write occurring.
</p>

<h2>3. System Architecture Specification</h2>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Layer</th>
      <th>Technology</th>
      <th>Operational Role</th>
      <th>Security / Integrity Protocol</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Frontend</strong></td>
      <td>React 18, Vite 5, Tailwind v4</td>
      <td>Single-page progressive operational terminal</td>
      <td>Liquid glassmorphism, responsive viewports (Mobile, Tablet, Laptop)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Backend API</strong></td>
      <td>Node.js 22, Express.js 5</td>
      <td>RESTful transaction gateway &amp; WebSocket server</td>
      <td>Zod validation schemas, strict JWT claim verification</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Database</strong></td>
      <td>PostgreSQL 15 / 16</td>
      <td>Primary ACID relational storage engine</td>
      <td>Pessimistic row-level locking (<code>FOR UPDATE</code>), Foreign Key cascades</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>ORM</strong></td>
      <td>Prisma ORM 6</td>
      <td>Type-safe query generation &amp; database migrations</td>
      <td>Automatic transaction wrapper (<code>$transaction</code>)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Cache &amp; Telemetry</strong></td>
      <td>Redis 7 &amp; Socket.io 4</td>
      <td>Real-time inventory broadcast &amp; catalog acceleration</td>
      <td>Sub-millisecond query caching, bi-directional live events</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>4. Database Schema &amp; Data Dictionary</h2>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Table Name</th>
      <th>Primary Key</th>
      <th>Foreign Keys</th>
      <th>Core Attributes &amp; Purpose</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>User</code></td>
      <td><code>id (UUID)</code></td>
      <td><code>roleId</code>, <code>warehouseId</code></td>
      <td>Stores user email, legal name, avatar, account status (<code>ACTIVE</code>, <code>PENDING</code>).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>Role</code></td>
      <td><code>id (UUID)</code></td>
      <td>None</td>
      <td>System clearance designations (<code>GLOBAL_ADMIN</code>, <code>NODE_MANAGER</code>, etc.).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>Warehouse</code></td>
      <td><code>id (UUID)</code></td>
      <td>None</td>
      <td>Physical hub code (e.g. <code>DEL-01</code>), name, location, and maximum capacity limit.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>Product</code></td>
      <td><code>id (UUID)</code></td>
      <td>None</td>
      <td>Universal SKU identifier barcode, product title, base cost price, description.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>Inventory</code></td>
      <td><code>id (UUID)</code></td>
      <td><code>productId</code>, <code>warehouseId</code></td>
      <td>Tracks localized physical quantity with unique compound constraint <code>[productId, warehouseId]</code>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>AuditTransaction</code></td>
      <td><code>id (UUID)</code></td>
      <td><code>productId</code>, <code>warehouseId</code></td>
      <td>Immutable financial &amp; stock movement ledger (type: <code>INBOUND</code>, <code>OUTBOUND</code>).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>Supplier</code></td>
      <td><code>id (UUID)</code></td>
      <td><code>warehouseId</code></td>
      <td>Authorized procurement vendors mapped to specific logistics hubs.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

